{
  "id": "lost-kingdoms-2002",
  "kind": "game",
  "title": "Lost Kingdoms",
  "subtitle": "FromSoftware · GameCube",
  "summary": "Lost Kingdoms is FromSoftware's GameCube action role-playing game about Princess Katia searching for her father as a black fog consumes the kingdom of Argwyll. A Runestone allows her to use magical cards that summon creatures and attacks. She cannot simply meet a monster with her own sword, so the deck supplies the means of surviving each encounter. The familiar idea of collecting fantasy creatures becomes an immediate physical problem: Katia must keep moving while choosing what to summon and where its effect will be most useful.",
  "body": [
    "Lost Kingdoms is FromSoftware's GameCube action role-playing game about Princess Katia searching for her father as a black fog consumes the kingdom of Argwyll. A Runestone allows her to use magical cards that summon creatures and attacks. She cannot simply meet a monster with her own sword, so the deck supplies the means of surviving each encounter. The familiar idea of collecting fantasy creatures becomes an immediate physical problem: Katia must keep moving while choosing what to summon and where its effect will be most useful.",
    "Battles unfold in real time, requiring attention to the character's position as well as the selected card. Different creatures and elemental relationships provide different answers to an opponent, making preparation important before the encounter begins. New cards can be found, purchased or created through changes to the collection. The player develops a deck whose contents should work together once danger closes in. A powerful summon is useful only if it can be deployed at a moment and place where Katia can survive long enough to benefit from it.",
    "Kota Hoshino composed the music for a game released in 2002, with Activision publishing the overseas editions. A separate two-player mode lets opponents bring decks into direct competition under additional restrictions. Lost Kingdoms applies the studio's interest in dangerous fantasy environments to a particularly accessible central object: the hand of cards becomes the player's available response to the world. Its sequel followed in 2003, expanding a system that already made collecting and action closely dependent on one another rather than separate activities between battles."
  ],
